      "conditions":["Selecting reassessment or excluding checks after a material change."],
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":856,
          "quote":"Excluding a check requires supported dependency coverage and a reason the change\ncannot invalidate the reused claim. An absent edge proves no non-influence.\nUnknown impact widens assessment or leaves the dependent assurance unresolved.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FConstruction%20assurance%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20retention",
          "start_line":850
        }
      ],
      "key":"impact_selection_requirements",
      "statement":"A requirements-to-test link alone cannot establish complete impact. Excluding a check requires supported dependency coverage and a reason the change cannot invalidate its reused claim; absent edges prove no non-influence. Unknown impact widens assessment or leaves dependent assurance unresolved. New obligations and uncovered cases require assessment even without an existing check; mandatory gates remain applicable. Selection produces no execution evidence. Consider impact-analysis, acquisition, execution and assessment costs together, distinguishing estimates from measurements; required assurance, risk policy and gates constrain selection, and low cost cannot leave required obligations uncovered. Broad/uncertain impact, changed selection model, release rule or missed dependency can justify wider testing; full-suite execution can still have gaps and implies neither universal cadence nor gate exemption.",
      "uncertainty":"No impact model, dependency coverage or concrete check outcome is supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["change_impact_evidence_retention"],
      "conditions":["Reusing evidence for a new subject or basis."],
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":878,
          "quote":"The original\nrecord keeps its original subject; an applicability relation permits its use\nfor the new claim. A changed digest proves neither invalidity nor equivalence.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FConstruction%20assurance%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20retention",
          "start_line":868
        }
      ],
      "key":"evidence_retention_requirements",
      "statement":"Retention establishes continuing claim/scope applicability, justified assertion and observation bases, required independence and unchanged material dependencies or an established preservation relation. Retain original subject and historical observation; use an applicability relation for the new claim. Changed support reopens affected reliance, not the historical observation; changed digest proves neither invalidity nor equivalence. Keep known failures, unresolved coverage and citations visible until supported repair, reassessment or authorised obligation change disposes their outstanding status; retain original observation, counterexample, subject, citations and reason for changed applicability. Relaxation can change what is owed but cannot convert or erase former failure. Assess changed subjects against current obligations and applicable evidence. Changed requirements revisit dependent expectations under their authority; changed refinements revisit requirement/realization links; changed realizations revisit affected implementation/integration under remaining obligations. Dependencies are semantic, not fixed file boundaries. Recovery acquires current reality and invalidates affected reliance without erasing valid work or requiring every check rerun; role, conversation, component or resumption boundaries alone do not invalidate. Account completeness differs from execution freshness. Failures enter diagnosis and authorised response; evidence acquisition may precede justified repair.",
      "uncertainty":"Independent-assessment and diagnosis/response owner clauses are referenced but not fully supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["workspace_context"],
      "conditions":["Acquiring or relying on context."],
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":920,
          "quote":"Absence from a partial\nprojection, sparse checkout or empty local record is not evidence of absence\nin the bound subject; acquire its authoritative source or retain indeterminacy.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FWorkspace%20and%20context",
          "start_line":918
        }
      ],
      "key":"acquisition_binding_requirements",
      "statement":"Observe the subject and basis actually bound by activation, including selected frozen snapshots. Admissible contrary current evidence reopens affected claims through drift/re-entry rules without silently changing basis. Current observations correct stale beliefs but do not permit requirement changes to match implementation. Bind current task, subject, destination constraints, authority and relevant observations; include known material dependencies directly or through declared available inputs; exclude irrelevant material unless needed to establish irrelevance. Rules without current subject, digests without accessible meaning and summaries without supported freshness are insufficient; summaries cannot override owners. Partial-projection absence requires authoritative acquisition or retained indeterminacy. Execution binding declares resource envelope and measurement boundary: byte/token counts establish fit only for measured context; actual use establishes attention adequacy. Fit does not prove adequate attention, successful tasks do not measure unobserved context, and no universal attention score follows.",
      "uncertainty":"The concrete activation, freshness support and measured context are unspecified."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["workspace_context"],
      "conditions":["Observing mutable subjects, resuming work or repeating operations with uncertain effects."],
      "exceptions":[],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":955,
          "quote":"Before repeating an operation with uncertain effects, observe or reconcile\nsufficiently to decide the next dependent action. A granted idempotent operation\nmay be retried where its idempotence is established for that subject and effect.\nOtherwise select another permitted action or return the unresolved result.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FWorkspace%20and%20context",
          "start_line":952
        }
      ],
      "key":"freshness_recovery_retry",
      "statement":"Bind observations to source and observation time/version. If change can occur before effect, supply material freshness/concurrency conditions by re-observation, revision comparison, protected snapshot or uncertainty; do not assume stale reads remain current, and no universal isolation scheme follows. Cycles use core results and can stop after any operation without invented completion. Discardable private context does not erase progress; resumption reconstructs sufficient context from continuing workspace and authoritative inputs without requiring every activation transcript. Reuse applies change-impact/evidence-retention to current subject, basis and changes; stored green labels are not validity arguments, and production/invalidation of relied-on support remain recoverable. Context or resumption boundaries alone prove neither applicability nor invalidity. Reconcile uncertain effects before dependent repetition; retry is permitted for a granted operation with established subject/effect idempotence, otherwise choose another permitted action or return unresolved. Retained partial progress does not make all operations idempotent; event logs can support observation, audit or domain reconstruction but are not universal work state or mandatory recovery algorithms.",
      "uncertainty":"The shared result algebra is referenced; its full operational definition is not supplied."
    },
    {
